node chat-mina-quiz
kind: reply
pose: explain

    A short round of Myth Buster on this unit.

    It's a gentle way to warm up.

link: Start the round => mythbuster radiation-and-the-body
reply: not now -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-worry
kind: reply
pose: think

    That's fair. A lot of people feel that way about radiation.

    What is it that worries you?

reply: it's everywhere -> chat-mina-w-everywhere
reply: is any amount deadly? -> chat-mina-w-deadly
reply: scary headlines -> chat-mina-w-headline
reply: never mind -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-w-everywhere
kind: reply
pose: explain

    It is around us all the time. That's true, and it's normal.

    In the United States the average person gets about 6.2 millisieverts a year, and most of it is natural background radiation.

link: Open it in AtomDex => entry background-radiation
reply: thanks, that helps -> chat-mina-menu
reply: where does it come from? -> chat-mina-w-where

node chat-mina-w-where
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Rocks and soil, space, and radon in the air all add to it.

    Every one of those is part of an ordinary year.

reply: ok, back -> chat-mina-menu
reply: that's reassuring -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-w-deadly
kind: reply
pose: explain

    No. Everyone gets small doses every day.

    It takes a very large dose in a short time, about 1000 millisieverts, to make people ill.

link: Open it in AtomDex => entry acute-radiation-syndrome
reply: thanks, that helps -> chat-mina-menu
reply: what about small doses? -> chat-mina-w-small

node chat-mina-w-small
kind: reply
pose: idle

    Low levels do not cause immediate health effects.

    They can add a small increase in the risk of cancer over a lifetime. A small chance, not a certainty.

reply: ok, back -> chat-mina-menu
reply: so it's a small chance -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-w-headline
kind: reply
pose: point

    A number in a headline needs three questions.

    How big is the dose? How fast did it arrive? And how does it compare with doses you know?

reply: that's a good list -> chat-mina-menu
reply: I'll try that -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-remind
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Which part would you like to go over?

reply: the dose scale -> chat-mina-r-scale
reply: cells and repair -> chat-mina-r-cells
reply: time, distance, shielding -> chat-mina-r-three
reply: never mind -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-r-scale
kind: reply
pose: explain

    1 sievert is 1000 millisieverts.

    An average year is about 6. A whole body CT is about 10. About 1000 in a short time is where people become ill.

link: Open it in AtomDex => entry millisievert
reply: thanks, back -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-r-cells
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Ionizing radiation can damage the DNA in a cell.

    Cells are extremely efficient at repairing that damage. If it is not repaired correctly, a cell may die or eventually become cancerous.

link: Open it in AtomDex => entry dna
reply: thanks, back -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-r-three
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Less time near a source, more distance from it, and something in between.

    Lead, concrete or water protect against gamma rays.

link: Open it in AtomDex => entry shielding
reply: thanks, back -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-talk
kind: reply
pose: greet

    Of course. Ask me anything.

reply: why the stethoscope? -> chat-mina-steth
reply: how do you stay calm? -> chat-mina-calm
reply: never mind -> chat-mina-menu

node chat-mina-steth
kind: reply
pose: idle

    It's a reminder.

    Listening first, then explaining. That's the order I like.

reply: I like that -> chat-mina-talk
reply: that's thoughtful -> chat-mina-talk

node chat-mina-calm
kind: reply
pose: proud

    Numbers help. When I know how big something is, and how it compares, it stops feeling so large.

    That's most of the unit, really.

reply: that makes sense -> chat-mina-talk
reply: I'll try it -> chat-mina-talk

node chat-mina-bye
kind: sign_off
pose: greet

    Of course. Go gently.

    Come back with any question you like.
